  "worked_solution": "Since $u(t)$ and $v(t)$ are solutions to ${x}^{\u2032}(t)=Ax(t),$ the superposition principle guarantees that \n\n$$\ncu(t)+dv(t)\n$$\n\n is also a solution to ${x}^{\u2032}(t)=Ax(t)$ for all $c,d\\in R.$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\nw(t)=u(t)-v(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $w$ is a solution to ${x}^{\u2032}(t)=Ax(t).$ So, in order to find $Aw(t)$ we just need to find ${w}^{\u2032}(t)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Aw & ={w}^{\u2032}(t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t-\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t)^{\u2032} \\\\ (\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t+\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t)^{\u2032}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t+\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t+\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t+\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}\u2061t-\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
